<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galicians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Galicians are the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, whose language, Galician, is closely related to Portuguese. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Galicia was part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and later of León, and its people and nobles played a large part in the resettlement of the lands to the south. The shrine of Saint James at Compostela made Galicia one of the great pilgrimage destinations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galicians.jpg" alt="Galicians" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Galician_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name galegos derives from the Latin Gallaeci or Callaeci, an adaptation of the name of a local Celtic tribe known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Kallaikoí. They lived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and northern Portugal and were defeated by the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimus-junius-brutus-callaicus">Decimus Junius Brutus Callaicus</a> in the 2nd century BCE and later conquered by Augustus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> then applied the name to all the people of the north-west who shared the same culture and language, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> valley in the south to the Cantabrian Sea in the north and west to the Navia River. The oldest known inscription naming them, found in 1981 at Aphrodisias in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, lists them among fifteen nations Augustus conquered.</p><p>Human occupation of the region is far older. The oldest human remains found, at Chan do Lindeiro, belong to a woman who lived some 9,300 years ago. About 6,500 years ago a new population arrived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with agriculture and husbandry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> became one of the foci of the Atlantic European megalithic culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, when Gallaecians were among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> hired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. After the conquest, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> was divided into three conventi and annexed to the province of Hispania Tarraconensis. Pliny listed the peoples of each, and several of their names survive in regional and village names such as Lemos, Trives and Valdeorras.</p><p>In 409 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> and Alans entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, and the province of Gallaecia was assigned to the Suebi and the Hasding Vandals. The two groups clashed in 419, and the Vandals left for southern Iberia and in 429 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 718 the area briefly came under Moorish control after the fall of the Visigothic realm, and the Galicians rebelled successfully in 739.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Galician, a Romance language derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of Roman Gallaecia, shares a common origin with Portuguese. Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a> developed a literary tradition from the late 12th century, and from the 15th century it diverged into Galician and Portuguese. The Royal Galician Academy regards modern Galician as an independent language closely related to Portuguese, particularly its northern dialects.</p><p>Today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> is an autonomous community of four provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">A Coruña</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>, Ourense and Pontevedra, and Galician and Spanish are official languages. In the 19th century writers such as Eduardo Pondal and Manuel Murguía led a Celtic revival, drawing on ancient authors, language, hill-forts and megaliths.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galicians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
